  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Daily severe weather threat through the end of \nthe week.\n\nMultiple rounds of thunderstorms, some likely severe with\ndamaging winds, are likely today through Friday as upper level\ndisturbances move from the Great Lks into the Mid Atlantic\nstates. Already seen a small cluster of thunderstorms over south\ncentral Pennsylvania that will move through the area later this\nmorning. Two additional rounds of thunderstorms seem likely\ntoday, one this afternoon, and a second one late this evening \ninto the overnight hours tonight into Thursday. Thunderstorms \nwill ride southeast along and north of a backdoor front \ncurrently pushing south through northern VA and southern MD as \nseen on TDWRs and wind shifts to the NE at IAD and DCA a couple \nof hours ago. The severe threat will not be necessarily tied to\nthe afternoon and evening hours as record warm nighttime \ntemperatures and high humidity yield strong instability and \nkeep thunderstorms going strong well into the overnight hours. \n\nBy Friday, cold front should have cleared much of the forecast \narea causing a stabilizing effect limiting the risk of severe\nweather, but showers and thunderstorms will still be possible\nnorth of the sfc front. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions yield likely record \nhigh minimum temperatures this week.\n\nDecent model agreement that areas south of US-33 and US-211 will\nsee heat indices meet heat advisory criteria this afternoon.\nBased on yesterday's high temperature readings and greater\nconfidence, issued Heat Advisories for the southern half of the\nfcst area. \n\nThursday's max temperature forecast has a lot more \nuncertainty due to potential for more convection. If convection\ndoesn't materialize, more record high minimums are possible\nagain today, but that doesn't seem likely at this time, except\nonly at Charlottesville. \n\nRecord high minimum temperatures were set yesterday at IAD, DCA,\nand HGR. RERs were issued earlier. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Heat & thunderstorms begin to decrease heading into \nLabor Day weekend.\n\nHigh pressure tracks southeast from the Great Lakes on Saturday, \npushing a cold front further south of the region. This should \nbring a drop in heat & humidity across the Mid-Atlantic, with \nafternoon highs ranging in the mid-80s on Saturday. As mid-level dry \nair continues to track across the area, temperatures should continue \nto decrease as the weekened goes on. By the Labor Day holiday, it \nshould feel relatively mild & dry, with dewpoints ranging in the \nupper-50s across the region. \n\nThunderstorm chances also begin to decrease past Friday once the \ncold front tracks to the south. A few showers & thunderstorms remain \npossible along the Blue Ridge as low-level moisture persists \nSaturday evening, but overall conditions should gradually clear as \nthe weekend progresses. Surface high pressure tracks overhead by \nMonday, further reducing precipitation chances over the holiday.",
